e a sunny line can meet at most one of them.\n   With only two sunny lines left one point stays uncovered.  \n   Consequently a configuration with three distinct lines and exactly two sunny\n   lines does **not** exist. Hence for \\(n=3\\)\n\n   \\[\n   k\\neq 2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix the non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{0}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   It covers the whole anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   For each \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose a distinct integer \\(t_{c}\\) with\n   \\(1\\le t_{c}\\le c-1\\) and define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{c}:\\text{ the line through }(t_{c},c-t_{c})\\in D_{c}\n   \\text{ and }(c-1,1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_{c}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{c}= \\frac{1-(c-t_{c})}{c-1-t_{c}}\n        =-\\frac{c-1-t_{c}}{c-1-t_{c}}=-1,\n   \\]\n\n   which would be forbidden; therefore we **modify** the construction as follows.\n   Choose a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) having no fixed point\n   (a derangement, which exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\text{ the line through }P_{i}=(i,n+1-i)\\in D_{n+1}\n   \\text{ and }Q_{i}=(\\sigma(i),1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\), the slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{i}= \\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{\\sigma(i)-i}= -\\frac{n-i}{\\sigma(i)-i}\n   \\]\n\n   is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.\n   Moreover the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   – The line \\(L_{0}\\) (3) covers all points of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   – For any \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the point \\((c-1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{c-1}\\); thus each\n     anti‑diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) contains at least one point already covered.  \n   – The second point of \\(L_{c-1}\\) is \\(P_{c-1}=(c-1,n+2-c)\\in D_{n+1}\\).  \n     The vector \\( \\overrightarrow{P_{c-1}Q_{c-1