            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad\nc=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe required set of points is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}D_{c},\n\\qquad |D_{c}|=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1\nA sunny line meets each \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_{c}\\) then\n\\(a+b=a'+b'=c\\) and the slope would be \\(\\dfrac{b'-b}{a'-a}=-1\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),\\,(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).\nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can contain at most one of them; therefore at least one\nof the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The case \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\n\n\\[\nD_{2}=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\nD_{3}=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\nD_{4}=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line covering two points of the same \\(D_{c}\\) has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)\nand is therefore non‑sunny.  The three points of \\(D_{4}\\) can be covered only by\nthe non‑sunny line \\(x+y=4\\).  After using that line we have two lines left,\nboth of which must be sunny.  Each of the remaining three points belongs to a\ndifferent \\(D_{c}\\); a sunny line can meet at most one of them (Lemma 1), so two\nsunny lines cannot cover all three.  Consequently a configuration with three\ndistinct lines and exactly two sunny lines does not exist.  Hence for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\neq2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (valid for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nChoose a **derangement** \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (i.e. a permutation\nwith \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) for every \\(i\\); such a permutation exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\)).  Define the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* \\(L_{0}: x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence non‑sunny);\n* for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  L_{i}:\\text{ the line through }P_{i}=(i,n+1-i)\\in D_{n+1}\n  \\text{ and }Q_{i}=(\\sigma(i),1).\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) equals\n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{\\sigma(i)-i}= -\\frac{n-i}{\\sigma(i)-i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\).  Hence each\n\\(L_{i}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are distinct.\n\n**Coverage of the points.**  \n\n*The anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).*  \nAll its points are the \\(P_{i}\\) and are covered by the lines \\(L_{i}\\) together\nwith \\(L_{0}\\).\n\n*An arbitrary diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\).*  \nConsider the line \\(L_{c-1}\\).  It contains the point \\(Q_{c-1}=(\\sigma(c-1),1)\\)\nand the point \\(P_{c-1}=(c-1,n+2-c)\\).  The vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(\\sigma(c-1)-(c-1),\\;1-(n+2-c))\n      =(\\sigma(c-1)-c+1,\\;c-n-1)\n\\]\n\nhas coprime integer components (otherwise \\(\\sigma(c-1)=c-1\\), contradicting the\nderangement).  Therefore the lattice points on the segment \\(P_{c-1}Q_{c-1}\\) are\nexactly the integer points\n\n\\[\n(a,c-a),\\qquad a=2,3,\\dots ,c-1 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with the endpoint \\(Q_{c-1}=(c-1,1)\\) we obtain **all** points of\n\\(D_{c}\\).  Consequently each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) is completely\ncovered by the single sunny line \\(L_{c-1}\\).\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P\\).  Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny\nlines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines\n\nStarting from the configuration of §3, replace any \\(t\\) of the sunny lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) by the non‑sunny diagonal line that contains the\nanti‑diagonal they were covering (i.e. replace \\(L_{c-1}\\) by the line\n\\(x+y=c\\)).  Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while\npreserving the covering property, because the replaced line already covered\nexactly the same set of points.  By choosing \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain\nconfigurations with exactly\n\n\\[\nk=n-1-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  For \\(n=3\\) the case \\(t=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=2\\)) is impossible by\n(8); all other values \\(k=0,1\\) are realised by the above replacement process.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility of all lines being sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covers\n\\(\\mathcal P\\).  By Lemma 1 each line meets \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, so\nthe \\(n\\) lines must contain exactly the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\nConsider the three points of \\(D_{3}\\): \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  Because a sunny line\