gma(c-1),1)\\)\nand the point \\(P_{c-1}=(c-1,n+2-c)\\).  The vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(\\sigma(c-1)-(c-1),\\;1-(n+2-c))\n      =(\\sigma(c-1)-c+1,\\;c-n-1)\n\\]\n\nhas coprime integer components (otherwise \\(\\sigma(c-1)=c-1\\), contradicting the\nderangement).  Therefore the lattice points on the segment \\(P_{c-1}Q_{c-1}\\) are\nexactly the integer points\n\n\\[\n(a,c-a),\\qquad a=2,3,\\dots ,c-1 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with the endpoint \\(Q_{c-1}=(c-1,1)\\) we obtain **all** points of\n\\(D_{c}\\).  Consequently each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) is completely\ncovered by the single sunny line \\(L_{c-1}\\).\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P\\).  Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny\nlines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines\n\nStarting from the configuration of §3, replace any \\(t\\) of the sunny lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) by the non‑sunny diagonal line that contains the\nanti‑diagonal they were covering (i.e. replace \\(L_{c-1}\\) by the line\n\\(x+y=c\\)).  Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while\npreserving the covering property, because the replaced line already covered\nexactly the same set of points.  By choosing \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain\nconfigurations with exactly\n\n\\[\nk=n-1-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  For \\(n=3\\) the case \\(t=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=2\\)) is impossible by\n(8); all other values \\(k=0,1\\) are realised by the above replacement process.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility of all lines being sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covers\n\\(\\mathcal P\\).  By Lemma 1 each line meets \\(