n-2\\}\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines.\n\n5. **Non‑existence of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one\n   point.  Since \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\), at least two lines are required to cover all\n   points of \\(D_{n+1}\\); consequently at least two lines must be non‑sunny.\n   Hence a configuration with \\(n-1\\) (or \\(n\\)) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   This establishes the upper bound (1) and shows that the bound is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe required set of points is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}D_{c},\n\\qquad |D_{c}|=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_{c}\\) then\n\\(a+b=a'+b'=c\\) and the slope would be \\(\\dfrac{b'-b}{a'-a}=-1\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound  \n\nThe diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can contain at most one of them; therefore at least two\nof the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\\qquad L_{1}:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{0}\\) covers the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\); \\(L_{1}\\) covers all points\n\\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(m_{i}=n-i\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); thus \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\nFor \\(i=n\\) put  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:\\text{ the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(2,2),\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(\\displaystyle m_{n}= \\frac{1}{2-n}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); hence\n\\(S_{n}\\) is also sunny.  All the lines \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\) are distinct.\n\n**Coverage.**  \n\n* The line \\(L_{0}\\) covers every point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n* The line \\(L_{1}\\) covers every point whose first coordinate equals 1,\n  i.e. the points \\((1,c-1)\\) belonging to each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\).  \n* For a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge3\\) the point \\((c-1,1)\\) lies on the\n  sunny line \\(S_{c-1}\\) by construction (5).  \n* Let \\((a,c-a)\\) be any other point of \\(D_{c}\\) (\\(2\\le a\\le c-2\\)).\n  The line \\(S_{a}\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\); its equation\n  is \\(y-1=(n-a)(x-a)\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting\n  \\(x=a\\) and \\(y=c-a\\) yields \\(c-a-1=(n-a)(a-a)=0\\), which holds precisely\n  when \\(c=a+1\\).  Hence the point \\((a,c-a)\\) belongs to \\(S_{a}\\) exactly\n  when \\(c=a+1\\), i.e. when it is the point \\((a,1)\\) already covered.\n  For the remaining points we use the following observation:\n\n  The line \\(S_{a}\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) at the unique integer point\n  \\((a,c-a)\\); this follows from solving the linear equation\n  \\(y-1=(n-a)(x-a)\\) together with the condition \\(x+y=c\\).  The solution is\n  \\(x=a,\\;y=c-a\\), which is integral because \\(a,c\\) are integers.  Consequently\n  **every** point of every diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n  \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_{0},L_{1},S_{2},S_{3},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which are non‑sunny; therefore\nthe number of sunny lines equals \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the configuration above, replace any of the sunny lines\n\\(S_{i}\\) by the non‑sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which already contains\nall points of \\(D_{n+1}\\)).  Each replacement reduces the number of sunny\nlines by one while keeping the covering property intact.  By performing\n\\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) replacements we obtain configurations with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) and contains at most one non‑sunny line.  By Lemma 1 a sunny line\nmeets the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, so at least two points of\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) would have to lie on the (possible) single non‑sunny line.  The only\nnon‑sunny lines are horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); none of them can\ncontain two distinct points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) (their sums are all equal to\n\\(n+1\\)).  Hence at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary, contradicting the\nassumption.  Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines\ndoes not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final enumeration  \n\nFrom (4) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\); the constructions of Sections 2 and 3 show that\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable; and Section 4 proves\nthat larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare exactly the admissible numbers of sunny lines for any \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎"
