e can contain at most one of them, therefore at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the following \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n   L_{2}:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\n   L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n}:\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points with integer first coordinate, while the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers the whole diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\). Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Construction for the maximal possible number of sunny lines when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the required points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\;x=1\\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}),\\qquad\n   L_{2}:\\;x+y=4\\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}),\\qquad\n   L_{3}:\\;y=x\\quad(\\text{sunny})\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct and together contain all six points. Thus for \\(n=3\\) the bound (1) is sharp: \\(k=n-2=1\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Open part.**  \n   The difficulty lies in extending the construction of §5 to arbitrary \\(n\\) so that exactly \\(n-2\\) of the lines are sunny while still covering every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\). A systematic method for doing this (and consequently for obtaining every intermediate value \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) has not been found.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nWe have proved that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe have exhibited configurations achieving \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k=n-2\\) for the smallest case \\(n=3\\).  \nThe existence of configurations with an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) remains an open 