onal, while a horizontal or vertical line covers at\nmost one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  Consequently we need at least one\nnon‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\); together with Lemma 2 this gives  \n\n\\[\nu\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad s\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe show that the bound (2) is sharp; i.e. for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there\nexists a configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{h}:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad L_{v}:\\;x=1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth are distinct and together they contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\nwith \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).\n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{-\\, (n-3),\\;-(n-5),\\dots ,\\,n-3\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(the set has exactly \\(n-2\\) elements) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); they are pairwise\ndifferent because their intercepts are different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Points of the large diagonal  \n\nFor the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) we have \\(c=n+1\\).  \nSince the numbers in (4) have the same parity as \\(n+1\\), for each\n\\(t\\) the intersection of \\(S_{t}\\) with \\(D_{n+1}\\) is the integer point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,b\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1-t}{2},\\frac{n+1+t}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nand the two coordinates satisfy \\(2\\le a,b\\le n-1\\).  \nHence the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(S_{t}\\) give **all** points of the diagonal\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) that are not already on \\(L_{h}\\) or \\(L_{v}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Points of the remaining diagonals  \n\nLet \\(c\\) be any integer with \\(4\\le c\\le n\\).  \nBecause \\(|t|\\le n-3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n-(c-4)\\le t\\le c-4 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(t\\) and \\(c\\) have the same parity exactly when (6) holds.\nConsequently the set of admissible \\(t\\)’s in (4) that satisfy (6) has\ncardinality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{c-4-(-(c-4))}{2}+1=c-3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(t\\) the intersection point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,b\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{c-t}{2},\\frac{c+t}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nlies on the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) and satisfies \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).\nThus the \\(c-3\\) lines \\(S_{t}\\) with admissible \\(t\\) give **all**\npoints of \\(D_{c}\\) that are not on \\(L_{h}\\) or \\(L_{v}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Completion of the covering  \n\nThe two lines (3) cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with\n\\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\); the family \\(\\{S_{t}\\}_{t}\\) covers all remaining\npoints, i.e. all points with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\n\\{L_{h},L_{v}\\}\\cup\\{S_{t}\\}_{t}\n\\]\nsatisfy the required covering property and contain exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  All intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe have shown that \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\nIf we replace any of the sunny lines \\(S_{t}\\) by an arbitrary\nnon‑sunny line (for instance a vertical line \\(x=2,3,\\dots\\)),\nthe covering property is preserved because the points that were on the\nremoved sunny line are still covered by the two axis lines or by the\nnew vertical line.  Repeating this replacement we can obtain configurations\nwith any number  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nof sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, the maximal number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\); the lower\nbound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is trivial.  The construction above proves that every\ninteger between these extremes can indeed be realised. ∎"
