\n   Once the lines are fixed on the points of \\(D_{k+1}\\), Lemma 1 guarantees that each line meets every other diagonal \\(D_{c}\\;(2\\le c\\le k)\\) in **exactly one** point (the intersection exists because the slopes are different from \\(-1\\)). Consequently each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) is hit by all \\(k\\) lines, and because \\(|D_{c}|=c-1\\le k\\) every point of \\(D_{c}\\) can be assigned to a distinct line among the \\(k\\). Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover the whole triangle \\(\\mathcal T_{k}\\).\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * For a given \\(n\\ge 4\\) and a chosen \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) we take  \n\n     * the diagonal line \\(L_{0}:x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny);\n     * the \\(n-1-k\\) further non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(x+y=n,\\dots ,x+y=n+2-k\\);\n     * the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of Step 6.\n\n     The total number of lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     1+(n-1-k)+k=n,\n     \\]\n\n     exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   * For \\(k=0,1\\) the explicit constructions of Step 4 work.\n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\mathcal P\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n     The argument of Step 3 shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible, while the constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) are immediate.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary conditions (1) and (2) together with the constructions of Sections 4 and 7 give a complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
