ing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe proceed by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.  The following four families of three lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | lines (all distinct) | sunny? |\n|------|----------------------|--------|\n| 0 | \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;y=2x-1\\) | one (the last) |\n| 2 | \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\\) | two |\n| 3 | \\((1,1)-(2,2),\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\;(1,3)-(2,1)\\) (slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) | three |\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge 3\\); i.e. for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(n=m+1\\) and fix a number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n- If \\(k\\le m-1\\) we take the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_{m,k}\\) (which has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines) and **add** the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is not sunny.  \n  The points of \\(S_n\\setminus S_m\\) are precisely the \\(n\\) lattice points satisfying \\(a+b=n+1\\); they all lie on \\(L\\).  Consequently the enlarged family \\(\\mathcal F_{m,k}\\cup\\{L\\}\\) consists of \\(m+1=n\\) distinct lines, covers the whole set \\(S_n\\), and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n- If \\(k=m\\) (the only remaining case, because \\(k\\le n-1=m\\)), we start from the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_{m,m}\\) that has **all** its \\(m\\) lines sunny and covers \\(S_m\\).  Adding the non‑sunny line \\(L\\) of (3) would give \\(k=m\\) sunny lines among \\(n=m+1\\) lines, which is precisely the required situation for the present value of \\(k\\).  Hence the family \\(\\mathcal F_{m,m}\\cup\