**Existence of configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).**  \n\n   *Base covering of the three sides.*  \n   The three non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   cover all points of \\(B_{n}\\).  After removing these three lines the uncovered set is the interior triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\) points.\n\n   *A universal sunny covering of the interior.*  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following three sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_{1}&:\\; y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_{2}&:\\; y = -\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_{3}&:\\; y = -2x+n+2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Because \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   One checks directly that exactly one of the three equalities  \n\n   \\[\n   b=a,\\qquad b=-\\tfrac12 a+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\qquad b=-2a+n+2\n   \\]\n\n   holds; indeed the three lines are the three sides of the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) after an affine transformation, and the integer points of \\(T_{n}\\) lie on one of its three edges.  Consequently every interior point belongs to at least one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).\n\n   The slopes of the three lines are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are sunny, and they are clearly distinct.\n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,3}= \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers the whole set \\(S_{n}\\), and contains exactly three sunny lines.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Fewer sunny lines.*  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_{n,3}\\) we may replace any sunny line \\(\\