tion assuming the bound, but note that the later conclusion does not follow.)\n\n---\n\n### 6. Derivation of inequality from (1)\n\n> “Since all points of \\(B_{n}\\) must be covered, (1) gives  \n> \\[\n> t+5n-6\\ge 3n-3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t\\ge -2n+3 .\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:** Algebraically correct. However, as noted above, the premise (1) is not justified, so the inequality does not provide a valid restriction on \\(t\\). The conclusion “\\(t\\ge -2n+3\\)” is trivial and does not bound \\(t\\) from above. **No new error, but the step rests on the previous gap.**\n\n---\n\n### 7. Corner‑point argument\n\n> “The three corner points \\((1,1),\\;(1,n),\\;(n,1)\\) lie on three different families … A sunny line can contain **at most one** of these corners, whereas a non‑sunny line can contain at most one corner as well … Consequently the three corners require **three distinct lines**.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Each corner lies on a different family (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).  \n- No line (sunny or non‑sunny) can contain two of these corners because they are not collinear.  \n- Therefore at least three distinct lines are needed to cover the three corners. This reasoning is sound. **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 8. Argument that \\(t\\ge 4\\) leads to a contradiction\n\n> “If \\(t\\ge 4\\) then at least four distinct lines are sunny; the three corners can be covered by at most three of them, so at least one sunny line would be forced to contain **no** corner.  \n> The remaining \\(n-4\\) lines (whether sunny or not) together with the three corner‑covering lines give at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines that meet the three sides, contradicting the fact that the three sides contain \\(3n-3\\) distinct points and each line can meet a side in at most one point unless it coincides with that side.  \n> Because only three lines can coincide with the three sides, we would need at least \\(3+(n-4)=n-1\\) distinct lines **in addition** to the three corner‑covering lines, i.e. at least \\(n+2\\) lines in total – impossible.”\n\n**Issue (Critical Error):**  \n- The reasoning mixes several unrelated counts without a clear logical chain.  \n- It is not established that the “remaining \\(n-4\\) lines together with the three corner‑covering lines give at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines that meet the three sides.” In fact, each of the \\(n-4\\) remaining lines could intersect a side at a new point, providing up to \\(n-4\\) additional distinct intersection points per side, far exceeding the claimed bound.  \n- The deduction that “we would need at least \\(n+2\\) lines in total” is not derived from any rigorous inequality; it rests on the flawed premise that each side can contribute at most one new point per line unless the line coincides with the side, which ignores the possibility that a line may intersect a side at a point already used by another line.  \n- Consequently the conclusion “\\(t\\le 3\\)” is not justified. This step invalidates the claimed upper bound on the number of sunny lines. **Critical Error.**\n\nBecause this error breaks the logical chain, any later conclusions that depend on the bound \\(t\\le 3\\) are unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Construction of three sunny lines covering the interior (Equation (4))\n\n> “For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following three sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\):  \n> \\[\n> \\ell_{1}: y = x,\\qquad\n> \\ell_{2}: y = -\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{2},\\qquad\n> \\ell_{3}: y = -2x+n+2 .\n> \\]”\n\n**Issue (Critical Error):**  \n- The set \\(T_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\\) contains many interior lattice points that are **not** collinear with any of the three lines listed.  \n- The claim “exactly one of the three equalities holds” is false; for most interior points none of the three equations is satisfied.  \n- The statement “the three lines are the three sides of the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) after an affine transformation” is misleading: an affine transfor