"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the triangular lattice of \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to any of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound \u2013 at most three sunny lines.**  \n   *Boundary points.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   B_{n}= \\bigl(\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\\bigr)\\cap S_{n}\n   \\]\n\n   (the three sides of the triangle).  \\(|B_{n}|=3n-3\\) (the three corner points are counted once).  \n\n   *How many points of \\(B_{n}\\) can a line contain?*  \n\n   * a sunny line meets each side of the triangle in at most one point, hence it contains at most three points of \\(B_{n}\\);  \n   * a non\u2011sunny line that coincides with a side contains all \\(n\\) points of that side; any other non\u2011sunny line meets a side in at most one point, therefore it contains at most two points of \\(B_{n}\\).\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   Suppose a configuration uses \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(s=n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The maximal number of points of \\(B_{n}\\) that can be covered is\n\n   \\[\n   3t\\;+\\;3\\cdot n\\;+\\;2(s-3)\n   =3t+3n+2n-2t-6\n   =t+5n-6 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (the term \\(3n\\) corresponds to the three possible side\u2011coincident non\u2011sunny lines, the term \\(2(s-3)\\) to the remaining non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   Since all points of \\(B_{n}\\) must be covered, (1) gives\n\n   \\[\n   t+5n-6\\ge 3n-3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t\\ge -2n+3 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality is always satisfied, so it does not yet bound \\(t\\).  \n   The decisive observation is that the three corner points\n   \\((1,1),\\;(1,n),\\;(n,1)\\) lie on three different families\n   \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\).  \n   A sunny line can contain **at most one** of these corners, whereas a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one corner as well (the three corners are not collinear).  \n   Consequently the three corners require **three distinct lines**.  \n\n   If \\(t\\ge 4\\) then at least four distinct lines are sunny; the three corners can be covered by at most three of them, so at least one sunny line would be forced to contain **no** corner.  \n   The remaining \\(n-4\\) lines (whether sunny or not) together with the three corner\u2011covering lines give at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines that meet the three sides, contradicting the fact that the three sides contain \\(3n-3\\) distinct points and each line can meet a side in at most one point unless it coincides with that side.  \n   Because only three lines can coincide with the three sides, we would need at least \\(3+(n-4)=n-1\\) distinct lines **in addition** to the three corner\u2011covering lines, i.e. at least \\(n+2\\) lines in total \u2013 impossible.  \n\n   Hence a configuration with \\(n\\) lines can contain **no more than three sunny lines**:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{t\\le 3}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence of configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).**  \n\n   *Base covering of the three sides.*  \n   The three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   cover all points of \\(B_{n}\\).  After removing these three lines the uncovered set is the interior triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\) points.\n\n   *A universal sunny covering of the interior.*  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following three sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_{1}&:\\; y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_{2}&:\\; y = -\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_{3}&:\\; y = -2x+n+2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Because \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   One checks directly that exactly one of the three equalities  \n\n   \\[\n   b=a,\\qquad b=-\\tfrac12 a+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\qquad b=-2a+n+2\n   \\]\n\n   holds; indeed the three lines are the three sides of the triangle \\(T_{n}\\) after an affine transformation, and the integer points of \\(T_{n}\\) lie on one of its three edges.  Consequently every interior point belongs to at least one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).\n\n   The slopes of the three lines are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are sunny, and they are clearly distinct.\n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,3}= \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers the whole set \\(S_{n}\\), and contains exactly three sunny lines.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Fewer sunny lines.*  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_{n,3}\\) we may replace any sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) by a non\u2011sunny line of the same family that already appears in (3).  \n   For instance, replace \\(\\ell_{1}\\) by the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\); this line contains all points of \\(T_{n}\\) that were on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and therefore does not destroy the covering property.  Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  Performing \\(3-k\\) such replacements yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Equation (2) shows that no configuration can have more than three sunny lines, while the constructions above exhibit configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)."
